      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "While laparoscopic appendectomy (LA) has become established in the diagnosis and treatment of acute appendicitis, its utilisation compared to open appendectomy (OA) is variable."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "AIM",
          "content": "To compare the utilisation and outcome of laparoscopic (LA) versus OA in an Irish regional hospital setting."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHOD",
          "content": "Retrospective review of OA and LA performed from 2003 to 2005."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Intention-to-treat analysis of 787 patients in this study revealed that 149 patients (19%) had LA and 638 patients (81%) had OA. Consultants were significantly more likely than trainees to undertake a LA (P < 0.0001). Twenty-two complications (2.8%) were recorded in the post-operative period. The overall negative appendectomy rate by histopathology was 17% with no significant difference between the rate in the LA group (19%) and the OA (17%) group."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Mean length of stay and complication rate were comparable between the LA and OA groups."
        }
      ]},
